WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.177
Can a compact mathematical representation recognise familiar people?

2
00:00:04.863 --> 00:00:10.069
I adapted an Eigenfaces workflow to photographs of people I know, connecting linear algebra

3
00:00:10.069 --> 00:00:12.076
with a practical recognition task.

4
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.424
The workflow prepares consistent grayscale images and subtracts the training mean face.

5
00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:24.001
Principal component analysis then learns directions of variation, giving each image a smaller

6
00:00:24.001 --> 00:00:25.279
set of coefficients.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.852
A test image is projected into the same learned space.

8
00:00:30.538 --> 00:00:35.154
A one-nearest-neighbour classifier assigns the identity of the closest training example.

9
00:00:35.841 --> 00:00:38.625
Reconstruction and recognition are checked separately.

10
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:43.768
I replaced the reference dataset with photographs of people around me.

11
00:00:44.454 --> 00:00:48.220
Recognition worked in this trial, with qualitatively similar results.

12
00:00:48.908 --> 00:00:52.905
A separate numerical evaluation of my custom dataset is still needed.

13
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.656
The original ORL report records thirty-eight correct predictions out of forty test images, or

14
00:00:59.656 --> 00:01:01.044
ninety-five percent.

15
00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.565
The matrix shown here is that reference result, not a measurement of my custom photographs.

16
00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:12.988
The project connects preprocessing, dimensionality reduction, and classification.

17
00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:18.933
Next steps are to document the custom split, repeat evaluation, and test lighting changes.

18
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.124
Read the full case study on my portfolio.
